WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:10.815
Python applications often connect user-controlled data to powerful
interpreters: database engines, operating system shells, template engines,
expression evaluators, and plugin systems.

2
00:00:10.815 --> 00:00:13.321
The mistake is not using these capabilities.

3
00:00:13.321 --> 00:00:19.388
The mistake is allowing untrusted or poorly constrained data to influence
instructions rather than values.

4
00:00:19.388 --> 00:00:26.445
When code builds commands or queries as strings, it becomes harder to
prove that data remains data and control remains control.

5
00:00:26.445 --> 00:00:33.039
For databases, safer patterns include parameterized queries, prepared
statements, and careful use of ORM features.

6
00:00:33.039 --> 00:00:37.392
An ORM can reduce risk, but it does not make every query pattern safe by
default.

7
00:00:37.392 --> 00:00:43.854
Raw query escape hatches, dynamic filters, string-built clauses, and
flexible search features still need review.

8
00:00:43.854 --> 00:00:50.449
The stable principle is to use APIs that separate query structure from
data, then review any exception with extra care.

9
00:00:50.449 --> 00:00:53.021
Command execution needs the same discipline.

10
00:00:53.021 --> 00:00:59.022
Many tasks are safer when handled by Python libraries instead of shelling
out to operating system commands.

11
00:00:59.022 --> 00:01:09.112
When subprocess behavior is required, developers should understand the
difference between passing controlled argument lists and invoking a shell
that interprets a command line.

12
00:01:09.112 --> 00:01:12.937
Untrusted values should not be allowed to become command structure.

13
00:01:12.937 --> 00:01:20.521
Allowlists, constrained options, safe working directories, clear timeouts,
and careful error handling are part of defensive design.

14
00:01:20.521 --> 00:01:29.226
Dynamic execution features such as eval, exec, dynamic imports, plugin
loading, and server-side template evaluation should be rare and heavily
reviewed.

15
00:01:29.226 --> 00:01:35.886
They can be useful in constrained systems, but they increase the cost of
reasoning about security and maintainability.

16
00:01:35.886 --> 00:01:43.932
If a feature needs dynamic behavior, prefer narrow interfaces, explicit
allowlists, data-driven configuration, and safe evaluation models.

17
00:01:43.932 --> 00:01:48.680
Clever code is not valuable if the team cannot secure it, test it, and
explain it later.
